```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch0
diagnosis_text:
The reported premise — that router0 is down — is incorrect. Router0 is fully
operational: all three interfaces (eth0=192.168.1.1/24 toward the IT LAN,
eth1=192.168.2.1/24 toward the CS LAN, eth2=10.10.0.1/8 WAN) are UP and
carrying traffic normally. RIP is configured and running.

The actual fault is on switch0, the L2 bridge that sits between the IT
department hosts (lan_it) and router0's eth0 (link_r0_sw0). switch0 uses a
Linux bridge (br0) to join those two collision domains. Although both physical
ports eth0 and eth1 are UP at the hardware level, the bridge device itself was
never brought up — its link state is DOWN (flags show only BROADCAST,MULTICAST,
with no UP or LOWER_UP). As a direct consequence, Linux STP marks both bridge
ports as "disabled", which means the bridge forwards no frames whatsoever.

When any IT host (e.g., it_hod at 192.168.1.2) attempts to ARP for the gateway
192.168.1.1, the ARP request cannot cross switch0, so router0 never sees it and
never replies. The ARP entry stays perpetually incomplete, producing
"Destination Host Unreachable" for every ping — which users interpret as the
gateway being dead.

The contrast with switch1 (CS side) confirms the isolation: switch1's br0 is UP
and both its ports are forwarding normally, which is why the CS department is
unaffected.

Root cause: switch0's bridge interface br0 was left in the administratively down
state, silently black-holing all L2 traffic between the IT LAN and router0.

Corrective fix: on switch0, run `ip link set br0 up`. The bridge will come up,
STP will transition the ports to forwarding, and IT department connectivity will
be immediately restored. To make this persistent across restarts, ensure the
startup script for switch0 includes `ip link set br0 up` after the bridge and
port assignments are configured.
=== END ===
```